<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maha Thammaracha III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1419">1419</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Sai Lue Thai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maha Thammaracha III, also known as Sai Lue Thai, was king of Sukhothai from about 1398 to 1419, as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>. He tried to restore Sukhothai's standing and strained against its overlord, but lacked the strength to break free, and the centre of the kingdom shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>. His death was followed by a succession dispute settled by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intharacha">Intharacha of Ayutthaya</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Sai Luthai about 1380, he was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammaracha-ii">Maha Thammaracha II</a> and a great-great-grandson of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a>. His mother was a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-thai">Li Thai</a>, Maha Thammaracha I, and was married to her own brother, Maha Thammaracha II. Much of what is known of the king comes from stone inscriptions that she had written in his name, among them the Asokārāma inscription and inscription No. 46, in which she bears long royal titles. He had a full brother, Prince Asoka, of whom nothing more is known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to inscription No. 46, the Queen Mother and her son, styled Mahādharrmarājādhipati Śrī Surīyavaṅsa, recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-bang">Phra Bang</a>, the present-day Nakhon Sawan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrae">Phrae</a> with her army in 1400, and then enjoyed dominion over all of Sri Satchanalai and Sukhothai. Holding Phra Bang interrupted the north-south transport of goods from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p><p>After the death of King Saen Mueang Ma of Lan Na, the kingdom to the north, in 1401, Sai Luthai intervened in the succession dispute on the side of the late king's eldest son, Thao Yi Kum Kam, and marched into Lan Na. Having seen a bad omen, he withdrew, taking Yi Kum Kam with him and giving him one of his provinces, possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamphaeng-phet">Kamphaeng Phet</a>, as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>, while Lan Na passed to Yi Kum Kam's younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-fang-kaen">Sam Fang Kaen</a>.</p><p>When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intharacha">Intharacha</a> came to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1409, one of his first acts was to reassert Ayutthaya's hold on Sukhothai as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state. The date is unknown, but inscription 49 was written in 1412 by Nai Inthara Sorasak, probably the resident sent by the king of Ayutthaya to watch over his vassal. On Maha Thammaracha III's death in 1419 the throne passed to his son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammaracha-iv">Maha Thammaracha IV</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maha Thammaracha III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
